<h1>Creating Your Own Noodles Adventure with AI</h1>
<p>
    Turn a few clear photos of your own plush character and a simple idea into a
    short, illustrated adventure of your own — no experience required. The tone here
    is the same one that built Wagglesworth Estate itself: encouraging, plainspoken,
    and honest about what needs fixing along the way.
</p>

<h2>The learning path</h2>
<ol>
    <li><strong>Your first adventure in 15 minutes.</strong> Choose one character, one place, one small problem, and one ending. Draft a short story and one illustration prompt.</li>
    <li><strong>Take useful reference photos.</strong> Front, side, close face, full body, and accessories, in neutral light against an uncluttered background. Never risk the toy, and never reveal private surroundings.</li>
    <li><strong>Make a character card.</strong> Record only visible facts and details you actually approve — nothing invented.</li>
    <li><strong>Brainstorm without losing your voice.</strong> Ask the AI for options, then choose and revise yourself. It's a helper, not the owner of your character.</li>
    <li><strong>Draft the story.</strong> Specify the audience, tone, length, plot beats, and which facts must never change.</li>
    <li><strong>Create scene prompts.</strong> Describe who, where, the action, the mood, the composition, and the palette — and say plainly what must stay unchanged.</li>
    <li><strong>Keep characters consistent.</strong> Reuse the same reference photos and the same immutable-details list every time. Change one thing at a time.</li>
    <li><strong>Fix a wrong result.</strong> Name the specific errors, ask for a limited correction, and keep everything else exactly as it was.</li>
    <li><strong>Write accessible descriptions.</strong> Describe the action and setting that actually matter to the story; mark purely decorative images as decorative.</li>
    <li><strong>Organize and publish safely.</strong> Keep your originals, use plain filenames, record which prompt made which image, get permission from anyone involved, and never publish private information.</li>
</ol>

